// probe: where does the engine find a plugin named by #pragma plugin nvmidi
//
// One line of script, one marker. This file is copied into each candidate
// location and run from there; whichever run prints LOC_OK is a location the
// engine accepts. Nothing else is in the file.
//
// What an exit code here means, corrected. This comment used to say an exit
// code was "a statement about the pragma resolution and nothing about the
// plugin's API". That is wrong, and run 36697433283 is what showed it: all
// four locations exited 70 with an empty stdout and "system exception" on
// stderr, which is a death *inside the plugin*, not a failure to find it.
// Resolution and load are one act - the engine locates the dll, calls its
// entry point, and the entry point is the plugin's own code (prepare_plugin,
// then the registrations). So this file does measure plugin code; it simply
// measures none of the plugin's *script-facing* API. The two are different
// questions and the header conflated them.
//
// The discriminator that makes 70 readable at all, measured on the same run:
// a pragma naming a plugin that does not exist exits 65 with 94 bytes on
// stdout and "ERROR: failed to load plugin". Absent gives 65 with a name;
// present-but-dying gives 70 with nothing. So the engine does find nvmidi.dll.
//
// fflush is not decoration. print() buffers, and the failure this file exists
// to observe kills the process before the buffer reaches the log - the same
// way logs_e2e.txt is 0 bytes in the e2e step (E2E_BEGIN is written and never
// flushed). Without the flush below, LOC_OK on a run that later dies would be
// indistinguishable from no LOC_OK at all.
#pragma plugin nvmidi

void main() {
	print("LOC_OK");
	fflush();
}
